  "topic_id": "yellow-alcohol-intoxication-severe-alcohol-withdrawal",
  "title": "Alcohol Intoxication / Severe Alcohol Withdrawal",
  "markdown": "# Alcohol Intoxication / Severe Alcohol Withdrawal\n\n> **PEARLS**\n> \n> Intoxicated patients with any of the following **MUST** be transported to the Emergency Department:\n> \n> **1) Incapacitating Intoxication:** inability to maintain airway; inability to stand from seated position and ambulate with minimal assistance; at immediate risk of environmental exposure or trauma due to unsafe location.\n> \n> **2) Acute Illness/Injury:** abnormal vital signs, physical complaint that may indicate underlying illness/trauma, seizure, hypoglycemia, trauma, head injury.\n> \n> Delirium tremens (DTs) is a severe form of alcohol withdrawal that can be life-threatening if not treated properly. DTs usually begin 48 hours after last alcohol consumption and is most severe 4-5 days after last alcohol consumption. Typical duration of DTs is 2-3 days but can last up to 8 days. Untreated DTs has a mortality rate of 37%. In contrast, hospitalized patients with DTs have a mortality rate of 1-4%.\n",
  "plain_text": "Alcohol Intoxication / Severe Alcohol Withdrawal PEARLS Intoxicated patients with any of the following MUST be transported to the Emergency Department: 1) Incapacitating Intoxication: inability to maintain airway; inability to stand from seated position and ambulate with minimal assistance; at immediate risk of environmental exposure or trauma due to unsafe location. 2) Acute Illness/Injury: abnormal vital signs, physical complaint that may indicate underlying illness/trauma, seizure, hypoglycemia, trauma, head injury. Delirium tremens (DTs) is a severe form of alcohol withdrawal that can be life-threatening if not treated properly. DTs usually begin 48 hours after last alcohol consumption and is most severe 4-5 days after last alcohol consumption. Typical duration of DTs is 2-3 days but can last up to 8 days. Untreated DTs has a mortality rate of 37%. In contrast, hospitalized patients with DTs have a mortality rate of 1-4%.",
